Workspace permission review — resolved

All four findings below were confirmed against the code and fixed. The rule they
all reduce to, and the reasoning, now live in
.plans/account_lifecycle_redesign_27ee88c8.plan.md, Phase 7:

  Workspace role decides whether you may write at all.
  The storage owner's account state decides which direction content may move.

Kept here as the record of what was actually wrong, since the plan describes the
destination rather than the starting point.

The invariant that was missing from the original write-up

gateStorageTx (storage.go) resolves the *owner's* AccountAccess and calls
CreateErr() before it ever looks at the byte counter. Every creation path
therefore already enforced owner lifecycle plus owner quota. That is what made
the two actor-level checks below purely additive, and it is why removing them
lost no enforcement.

1. Actor quota blocked some creation paths — FIXED

Only addSource (server.go) and generate (server.go) called
accountCreateAllowed(actor). Direct B2 upload, cloud import, note creation and
editor asset upload never did.

Two other actor checks were correct and stay: createEvent and CreateWorkspace
check the actor because events, tasks and workspaces are actor-owned — there the
actor *is* the storage owner.

addSource now has no actor check. generate calls the generationCreditsAllowed
stub, because generation's second cost is inference and that genuinely is the
actor's; see todo-llm-credits. accountCreateAllowed is deleted.

2. Actor quota restricted material updates — FIXED, and it was wider than
   described

The same requireAccountEdit(actor) gate sat on updateWorkspace,
updateWorkspaceSharing, transferWorkspace, updateEvent and updateTask, not just
updateMaterial.

The deeper problem: requireAccountEdit requires AccountActive, so an over-quota
owner could not rename or re-file their *own* material. That contradicts the
grace semantics ("no create/upload/clone; materials shrink-only; existing
content readable") regardless of who the actor is. Renaming is size-neutral and
is part of how an over-quota account finds what to delete.

Moved to requireAccountMutate: updateMaterial (except privacy), updateWorkspace,
updateEvent, updateTask, transferWorkspace. Transfer especially — handing a
workspace away is a recovery path, and the recipient is gated separately inside
TransferWorkspace.

Deliberately still strict: updateWorkspaceSharing, and the privacy field of
updateMaterial. Publishing puts content on Explore where every clone is charged
to the cloner, so it is not size-neutral in effect. updateMaterial picks its
gate from the patch.

3. Owner quota was not enforced before material growth — FIXED, reframed

The original framing ("the quota is enforced only after growth is recorded")
implies edits should be quota-gated. They deliberately are not: the storage plan
makes the quota a creation gate and bounds single-document growth with the 2 MiB
per-material cap instead. Arguing it as a missing gate invites the correct reply
"that's by design", and the real bug survives.

The real bug is narrower. Shrink-only exists precisely to stop an over-quota
account growing, and it was keyed on the connecting user. Wrong in both
directions: an over-quota editor got shrink in a healthy owner's room, and an
active editor got full write in an over-quota owner's room. ProjectMaterialContent
applies no state check of its own, so with the token minted from the wrong user
the chain was broken end to end.

createMaterialCollaborationToken now resolves store.MaterialOwnerAccess. A side
benefit: shrink-only becomes a property of the room rather than of each
connection, so two connections can no longer disagree about whether the document
may grow.

4. Generated flashcards had an unrelated permission mismatch — FIXED, and it was
   worse than described

CreateDeckWithCards resolved the workspace with WHERE id=$1 AND user_id=$2.
Three consequences beyond the one noted:

- It hit manual deck creation too. POST /api/decks had no membership check at
  all; it delegated authorization entirely to that predicate.
- The failure was a raw pgx.ErrNoRows, which is a distinct sentinel from
  store.ErrNotFound. Neither fail nor hErr matches it, so an editor got a 500
  with "no rows in result set" rather than a 403 or 404.
- CreateMaterial re-queries the workspace to resolve the owner anyway, so the
  lookup existed solely to enforce ownership.

Predicate removed, miss mapped to ErrNotFound, and createDeck now performs the
AssertWorkspaceEditor check that the generation path already did via assertWS.

Two bugs found while verifying the above, not in the original review

- Generated mindmaps, diagrams and quizzes never passed CreatedBy.
  CreateMaterial falls back to the workspace owner when it is empty, so work an
  editor did was recorded as the owner's, including in the first revision
  snapshot. Flashcards was the only generation path threading the actor through,
  which is exactly why it was also the only one tripping the ownership predicate
  above. Both fixed together.
- ListDecks set IsOwner = true on every row, including decks reached only
  through workspace membership, so a member would be shown owner-only
  affordances that the API then refuses. Now derived per row.

Surfacing the owner's state to the client

The gates above are correct but silent: a healthy editor in an over-quota
owner's workspace saw uploads and generation fail with nothing on screen tying
the refusal to an account that is not theirs. Workspace responses now carry
storageOwnerState and storageOwnerName — the state of the account charged for
the workspace's bytes, resolved per distinct owner — and WorkspaceOpen renders
StorageOwnerBanner above the centre pane naming whoever is out of space.

storageOwnerState is omitted rather than reported on Explore listings: the only
action there is a clone charged to the cloner, so the author's billing state is
both irrelevant and not the visitor's business. Absent therefore means "not
applicable", not "healthy".

Still missing on the frontend: the collaboration token's access: "shrink" has no
handling, so a member editing an over-quota owner's note sees the banner but
still gets a silent rejection when their edit grows the document.

Deferred

Live connections are not downgraded when their owner crosses into over-quota:
access is decided once in onAuthenticate and Hocuspocus never re-authenticates.
The five-minute token TTL bounds new connections only. Freeze is derived state,
so there is no transition event to evict from yet. Shape of the fix is in
todo-llm-credits.

